WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
BLACKOUTS:
Blackout. On June 12, 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:10.920
over 8,000 subreddits decided to go completely&nbsp;
dark. Users couldn’t join, edit, or even view any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.980
of the content on these subreddits. Instead, they&nbsp;
were met with messages like this. CFL has gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.800
private in protest against Reddit’s exorbitant new&nbsp;
API pricing. This community will not grant access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.640
requests during the protest. Please do not message&nbsp;
asking to be added to …oh...oops… definitely did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.920
not do that. Clearly, I was not aware of what&nbsp;
was going on but like hundreds of millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:40.440
other users, I became aware of the situation quite&nbsp;
quickly as some of the biggest communities were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:47.340
going down. This included r/aww with 34 million&nbsp;
members, r/music with 32 million members, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:54.360
r/futurology with 18 million members just to name&nbsp;
a few. At first, this was seen as one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.880
effective online protests we’ve seen as it not&nbsp;
only spread the word but it also made Reddit feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:05.220
the pain as they weren’t earning any ad revenue&nbsp;
from these subreddits. But, almost as quickly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:12.660
the protest gained steam, it disappeared. What did&nbsp;
Reddit do you ask? Well, the answer is nothing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:20.400
They just stood their ground and waited and things&nbsp;
have mostly returned to normal So, join me as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.160
take a deep dive into one of the biggest online&nbsp;
protests we’ve seen and why it utterly failed.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.540
WHAT’S THE BIG DEAL:
Alright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:40.860
so what’s the big deal anyway. Why are hundreds&nbsp;
of millions of users not able to see cat pics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:47.280
and memes? Well, it all boils down to the fact&nbsp;
that Reddit sucks, not as a platform though some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.740
would argue that as well, but as a business. We’ve&nbsp;
talked extensively on this channel about companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:59.040
prioritizing growth and influence over profits&nbsp;
but Reddit is unlike anything else we’ve seen. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:05.340
company has been around for nearly 20 years and&nbsp;
has 1.6 billion monthly active users. Put another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:11.220
way, it is the 19th most visited website on the&nbsp;
planet and the 12th most visited website in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:18.540
US but despite this, Reddit is somehow not only&nbsp;
not profitable but they pull in no money. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:24.480
Reddit is estimated to only pull in a mere $510&nbsp;
million per year which given their user count is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:31.500
absolute garbage. I mean, they’re literally making&nbsp;
2.5 cents per user per month. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.780
their own paid tier costs $6 per month, so&nbsp;
we’re talking about a revenue difference of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:43.200
nearly 200X between the average user and a paying&nbsp;
user. But, not only do they pull in no money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.680
they also spend a bunch of money. Maintaining a&nbsp;
platform with a billion plus users is not cheap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.720
by any means. You need massive server rooms,&nbsp;
databases, and lot of tech infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:00.540
in general. You also need a bunch of people to&nbsp;
maintain all this. And, being headquartered in San&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.760
Francisco, Reddit has become accustomed to insane&nbsp;
compensation packages of half a million dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.740
for software engineers which is great for the&nbsp;
engineers but once again garbage for the business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:18.660
Virtually no money coming in, exorbitant amounts&nbsp;
going out. To be honest, I have no idea how VCs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:24.240
have put up with Reddit’s financials for so long&nbsp;
but it looks like they’re finally pulling back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:30.240
In fact, Reddit is planning on IPOing later this&nbsp;
year likely hoping that the public markets are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.700
more tolerant of their losses. But before doing&nbsp;
that, they wanted to patch things up and make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.380
business seem as viable as possible. Likely&nbsp;
the easiest way to do this would’ve been to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:45.660
crack down on AdBlock. I don’t have any stats&nbsp;
for this but I’d wager that the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:51.360
of Redditors use AdBlock given that they tend to&nbsp;
be more tech savvy and rebellious in general. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.400
eliminating AdBlock itself might increase their&nbsp;
revenue by multiple fold and I’m sure they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:02.220
considered that. But, it looks like they decided&nbsp;
against it likely fearing public backlash. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:07.320
easy solution would’ve been to jack up prices&nbsp;
on their premium subscriptions. But, then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:13.920
Netflix’s brand favorability dropped to 11% after&nbsp;
doing the same thing. With the IPO coming up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.020
Reddit really couldn’t risk anything that would&nbsp;
harm the brand’s image. What they needed was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:25.020
revenue stream that no one would bat an eye about,&nbsp;
something that no one would even notice like API&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:32.280
usage. Surely, price hikes for API usage would&nbsp;
go completely unnoticed. Oh how wrong they were.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:40.200
SHOTS FIRED:
In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:45.720
late April, Reddit announced that they would start&nbsp;
charging for API usage for the first time. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:54.300
price $0.24 per 1000 API calls. Wait hold up what&nbsp;
even are API calls? Well, API calls are what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.300 --> 00:05:00.240
use to integrate various portions of Reddit into&nbsp;
your own website or app. API calls are not unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:06.780
to reddit. Likely the most popular APIs are Google&nbsp;
APIs. For example, if you go onto a restaurant’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.040
website and see an interactive map showing you&nbsp;
where the restaurant is located, that website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.860
is most likely using Google’s Map API. Similarly,&nbsp;
if you’re filling in a billing or shipping address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:23.160
and you see auto complete results, that website&nbsp;
is most likely using Google’s Places API. Every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.840
time a user interacts with the map or uses the&nbsp;
auto complete feature, an API call is being made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.240
to Google, and these websites are charged based&nbsp;
on how many API calls they make every month. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:40.440
dynamic maps, Google charges $7 per 1000 requests&nbsp;
and for Autocomplete, Google charges $2.83 per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:47.460
1000 requests. Considering this, Reddit’s $0.24&nbsp;
per 1000 calls is really not out of the ordinary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:53.220
Not to mention, Reddit is offering an extremely&nbsp;
generous free tier. In fact, you can make up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:58.800
100 calls per minute completely for free. So,&nbsp;
theoretically, you can make up to 4.3 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:06.300
calls per month for absolutely no cost, but then&nbsp;
what’s the issue? Well, the issue is that it used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:10.980
to be free regardless of scale meaning that&nbsp;
several substantial businesses have been built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:17.940
around this idea. One such business is Apollo.&nbsp;
Apollo is an iOS app that’s basically a skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:23.880
for Reddit. Essentially, it uses a bunch of Reddit&nbsp;
APIs to recreate the entire reddit experience but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:29.040
with a completely new UI which a lot of people&nbsp;
prefer. In fact, the app has nearly a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:35.940
daily active users. But, here’s the problem, apps&nbsp;
like Apollo don’t show Reddit ads because I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:40.920
what’s in it for them. This means that Reddit is&nbsp;
essentially having to support all of these users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:46.800
completely for free. Even worse, apps like Apollo&nbsp;
are able to make money by charging users a premium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:52.380
subscription fee none of which goes to Reddit.&nbsp;
So, these businesses basically have no expenses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:57.600
All of the backend infrastructure is maintained&nbsp;
and paid for by Reddit and all of the front end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:03.420
runs on iPhones, so the only expense for these&nbsp;
businesses is the cost of front end development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:09.480
And given that most of these apps are built by a&nbsp;
single developer or a small team, this is also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:15.180
non expense. So, basically everything that people&nbsp;
are willing to pay for these apps is pure profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:20.760
of course after the Apple tax. But, while this&nbsp;
adds up to a lot of money on an individual basis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:26.640
it’s not a lot to run an actual business of&nbsp;
their scale. For example, let’s assume that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:32.520
about 10% of Apollo’s user base actually pays for&nbsp;
the app. This would translate to approximately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:39.780
$130,000 per month which is again great as&nbsp;
an individual. But as a business with 900,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:46.320
active users making 7 billion API calls per&nbsp;
month, it’s nothing. And that’s the problem.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:57.060
THE BATTLE BEGINS:
The core of the Reddit protest is that API&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:03.420
pricing is simply way too high and on the surface&nbsp;
this indeed seems to be the case. For Apollo to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:09.540
continue operations, they would have to pay Reddit&nbsp;
$1.7 million per month or $20 million per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:17.640
That’s a crazy amount right? So, 24 cents per&nbsp;
1000 request must be unreasonable. But, here’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:23.820
the deal, Apollo is also making a crazy amount&nbsp;
of requests. They’re literally making 7 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:31.140
requests per month. That translates to nearly 8000&nbsp;
requests per user per month. That’s why it’s so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:37.380
expensive. It’s because the API is being used in&nbsp;
a way that it’s not meant to be used. Here’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:43.500
example of how APIs are normally used. Maybe, you&nbsp;
can integrate a twitter post into an article. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:49.200
would only cost a mere fifth of a penny per user&nbsp;
according to Reddit’s pricing. A super cheap fee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:55.680
But, apps like Apollo aren’t just importing one&nbsp;
post. They’re trying to import the entire platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:03.000
of Reddit and that’s why it adds up to $2.50 per&nbsp;
user per month. And if you’re still not convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:09.780
think about this. Even if Reddit decreased their&nbsp;
pricing by 10X down to 2.4 cents per 1000 calls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:15.960
that would still cost $170,000 per month for&nbsp;
Apollo which would still likely be unsustainable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:22.620
So, is this a case of Reddit implementing pricing&nbsp;
that’s magnitudes higher than it should be or is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:28.440
it it a case of apps like Apollo only being viable&nbsp;
if Reddit’s APIs are virtually free. I’ll let you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:33.360
be the judge of that. Feel free to post your&nbsp;
thoughts down below. But anyway, much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:39.060
userbases of Apollo, Reddplanet, Sync, and Reddit&nbsp;
is Fun naturally felt that it was Reddit’s fault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:42.780
and given that this emcompasses much&nbsp;
of Reddit’s most dedicated user base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:49.140
a lot of them knew mods or were mods themselves.&nbsp;
And it didn’t take them long to join forces and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:54.840
stage one of the largest online protests we’ve&nbsp;
seen dubbed Reddark. They would essentially make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:58.260
8000 of the most popular subreddits in&nbsp;
the world private starting on June 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:05.820
2023. And let’s just say, this definitely made a&nbsp;
scene. Almost overnight, all reddit users became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:11.040
aware of the protest against Reddit. And with how&nbsp;
quickly drama spreads, it wasn’t long until people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.500
who never use Reddit also knew about it. And given&nbsp;
that most people naturally have a tendency to side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:21.900
with the underdog, they immediately turned on&nbsp;
Reddit and especially Reddit’s CEO Steve Hoffman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:29.280
Steve was under a lot of fire but he didn’t budge&nbsp;
on Reddit’s stance whatsoever. The bottom line was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:34.800
“Reddit needs to be a self-sustaining business,&nbsp;
and to do that, we can no longer subsidize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:40.140
commercial entities that require large-scale data&nbsp;
use.” Reddit had made their position clear but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:45.480
this only infuriated the community even more.&nbsp;
They were gonna band together and protest for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:52.920
as long as it took. Wait nevermind, just 48 hours&nbsp;
later, most of these subreddits would be back up.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:11:03.060
THE UNCEREMONIOUS END:
You’re probably asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:08.280
what happened. Why did the protests die down&nbsp;
so quickly? Well, a lot of people point to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:13.680
limited blackout period of 48 hours. You see, in&nbsp;
order to get as many mods and subreddits on board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:19.920
the organizers of Reddark only asked for a 48 hour&nbsp;
blackout period. Further protesting would be up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:25.380
the discretion of the mods. So, people say that if&nbsp;
the mods were more committed, the protest would’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:30.720
been more effective which may have been true but&nbsp;
I don’t think it would’ve changed the end result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:37.020
all that much. The bottomline is that the average&nbsp;
Redditor simply did not care about protest more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:43.500
than simply saying “Oh wow, Reddit is bad”. Now,&nbsp;
where are my cat memes and Mr Robot discussion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:50.700
threads? I know that sounds stupid but that’s the&nbsp;
reality. Only 1 million people use Apollo. Over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:57.900
billion people use Reddit, so we’re talking about&nbsp;
0.1% of the userbase. So, while Reddark was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:02.160
to spread awareness about their campaign and even&nbsp;
get people to agree with the ideological effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:08.040
they were not able to get people to sacrifice&nbsp;
for the cause and that was really where it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:13.560
fell apart. The idea of any protest is that you&nbsp;
sacrifice things in the short term to make things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:19.020
better over the long term. The company Reddit&nbsp;
clearly understood this as that was exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:24.000
they were doing. By protesting the protest, they&nbsp;
were essentially sacrificing short term ad revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:30.180
and brand reputation for long term profitability.&nbsp;
Now, I do want to mention that a good amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:35.760
subreddits are still protesting but this is&nbsp;
increasingly becoming the minority especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:40.980
with pressure mounting against mods both from&nbsp;
the community and from Reddit. And unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:45.900
it looks like services like Apollo will indeed&nbsp;
be closing down at the end of June when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:51.600
pricing changes go into effect after which&nbsp;
protesting becomes virtually useless. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:56.100
company that might be worth protesting against&nbsp;
is Goodwill. Check out this video to learn more.

